    %% ---- Portfolio ----------------------------------------------------------
    %%entity Program help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. Everything else in this application ultimately hangs off a programme, and a programme that is discontinued takes its series and experiments out of the active portfolio with it.
    %%field Program.program_code help: The short internal code the programme is known by in every meeting and slide, such as ONC-114.
    %%field Program.name help: The readable name of the programme.
    %%field Program.therapeutic_area help: The broad disease area the programme sits in. Portfolio reviews are usually run one area at a time.
    %%field Program.indication help: The specific disease the medicine is intended to treat.
    %%field Program.stage help: Where the programme has reached, from picking a target to selecting a clinical candidate. Stage decides which data is expected to exist.
    %%field Program.status help: Whether the programme is active, paused, finished or stopped.
    %%field Program.target_id help: The biological target this programme acts on. One target per programme, by design — two targets is two programmes.
    %%field Program.owner_id help: The scientist accountable for the programme's science.
    %%field Program.started_on help: The date the programme was funded and work began.
    %%field Program.target_candidate_date help: The date the programme aims to nominate a clinical candidate. A plan, not a promise.
    %%field Program.annual_budget help: Approved spend for the current year, in the site's reporting currency.
    %%field Program.objective help: What the programme is trying to prove, in a sentence a new joiner could read.

    %%entity Target help: A protein or gene the programme intends to act on. The validation level is the most important thing on the record: a target nobody has validated in vivo is a much larger bet than one with clinical precedent, and the whole programme inherits that risk.
    %%field Target.gene_symbol help: The HGNC gene symbol, such as EGFR or BTK.
    %%field Target.uniprot_accession help: The UniProt accession for the protein, which is how this target is matched against external data.
    %%field Target.name help: The full protein name.
    %%field Target.target_class help: The structural family the target belongs to. It largely decides which screening approaches are worth trying.
    %%field Target.validation_level help: How strong the evidence is that acting on this target changes the disease — from a hypothesis to clinical precedent.
    %%field Target.is_antitarget help: Set when the target is one to avoid rather than to hit, such as hERG. Selectivity panels are built from antitargets.
    %%field Target.biological_rationale help: The argument for why this target matters in this disease.

    %%entity CompoundSeries help: A family of compounds sharing a chemical scaffold, explored together. Medicinal chemistry advances by series rather than by molecule, so this is the unit a portfolio review actually discusses — a series is progressed, deprioritised or abandoned as a whole.
    %%field CompoundSeries.series_code help: The short code the series is known by.
    %%field CompoundSeries.name help: A readable name for the series, usually naming the scaffold.
    %%field CompoundSeries.program_id help: The programme this series belongs to.
    %%field CompoundSeries.scaffold_smiles help: The core structure shared by the series, as SMILES.
    %%field CompoundSeries.status help: Whether the series is being actively worked, parked or dropped.
    %%field CompoundSeries.compound_count help: How many compounds have been made in this series so far.
    %%field CompoundSeries.design_hypothesis help: What the chemists believe this scaffold can be made to do, and what they are changing to find out.

    %% ---- Chemistry ----------------------------------------------------------
    %%entity Compound help: One registered chemical structure. A compound is registered once and measured many times; its calculated properties are computed on registration from the structure, while everything measured about it lives in AssayResult and AdmetProfile.
    %%field Compound.compound_number help: The internal registration number, the identifier the compound is known by everywhere else.
    %%field Compound.smiles help: The chemical structure in SMILES notation. This is the definition of the compound; everything else is derived from it or measured about it.
    %%field Compound.inchi_key help: The hashed structure identifier, computed on registration. It is what makes duplicate registration detectable.
    %%field Compound.common_name help: A human name for the compound where it has one.
    %%field Compound.cas_number help: The CAS registry number, where the compound is a known substance.
    %%field Compound.formula help: The molecular formula, computed from the structure.
    %%field Compound.compound_class help: What kind of molecule this is. Most discovery compounds are small molecules; fragments are handled differently because they are deliberately weak.
    %%field Compound.compound_series_id help: The chemical series this compound belongs to, where it belongs to one.
    %%field Compound.molecular_weight help: Molecular weight in daltons, computed from the structure.
    %%field Compound.logp help: Calculated lipophilicity. High logP buys potency and costs solubility, which is the oldest trade in medicinal chemistry.
    %%field Compound.tpsa help: Topological polar surface area, in square angstroms. It predicts whether a molecule can cross a membrane.
    %%field Compound.hydrogen_bond_donors help: Count of hydrogen bond donors, computed from the structure.
    %%field Compound.hydrogen_bond_acceptors help: Count of hydrogen bond acceptors, computed from the structure.
    %%field Compound.rotatable_bonds help: Count of rotatable bonds. More flexibility usually means a worse binder and worse oral absorption.
    %%field Compound.lipinski_violations help: How many of Lipinski's four rule-of-five criteria the compound breaks. Two or more is a warning rather than a verdict.
    %%field Compound.registration_status help: Where the compound is in registration. Only an approved compound may be screened.
    %%field Compound.registered_by_id help: The chemist who registered the structure.

    %% ---- Screening ----------------------------------------------------------
    %%entity Assay help: A defined way of measuring something about a compound. The definition is separate from the running of it because the same assay is run hundreds of times, and a result is only comparable with another when both came from the same assay definition.
    %%field Assay.assay_code help: The short code the assay is known by on plates and in reports.
    %%field Assay.name help: A readable description of what the assay measures.
    %%field Assay.assay_type help: Whether this is a purified-protein assay, a cell assay, a biophysical measurement, an ADMET endpoint, a selectivity panel or an animal study.
    %%field Assay.readout help: What the assay reports — a potency value, a percentage inhibition, a half-life, a permeability.
    %%field Assay.unit help: The unit the readout is expressed in. A number without its unit is not a result.
    %%field Assay.target_id help: The target this assay measures activity against, where it measures one.
    %%field Assay.protocol_reference help: A reference to the written protocol the assay follows.
    %%field Assay.acceptance_z_prime help: The lowest Z-prime a run of this assay may have and still be trusted. Below it, the plate is noise.
    %%field Assay.is_active help: Whether the assay is still available to be run.

    %%entity AssayRun help: One execution of an assay — typically one plate, on one instrument, on one day, by one operator. The run is where quality is judged: if its Z-prime is below the assay's acceptance threshold, every result on the plate is suspect no matter how good the numbers look.
    %%field AssayRun.run_reference help: The identifier for this run, used to trace a result back to the day and plate it came from.
    %%field AssayRun.assay_id help: The assay definition being executed.
    %%field AssayRun.experiment_id help: The experiment this run was scheduled under, where it was scheduled under one.
    %%field AssayRun.instrument_id help: The instrument that read the plate.
    %%field AssayRun.operated_by_id help: The person who ran the plate.
    %%field AssayRun.run_date help: The date the plate was run.
    %%field AssayRun.plate_barcode help: The barcode of the physical plate, which is how the run is matched to the raw instrument file.
    %%field AssayRun.z_prime help: The statistical quality of the plate, between minus infinity and 1. Above 0.5 is a good assay; below the assay's threshold the run is rejected.
    %%field AssayRun.qc_status help: Whether the run has passed quality control. Results from a failed run must not be used to make decisions.
    %%field AssayRun.status help: Where the run has got to, from scheduled to completed or failed.
    %%field AssayRun.retest_count help: How many times this run has been retested after a failure. A plate that keeps failing is an assay problem, not a compound problem.
    %%field AssayRun.failure_reason help: Why the run failed, where it did.

    %%entity AssayResult help: One compound's measurement from one run. This is the row the whole application exists to produce: a number, its unit, and enough context to know whether to believe it.
    %%entity AssayResult parent: AssayRun
    %%field AssayResult.assay_run_id help: The run this measurement came from.
    %%field AssayResult.compound_id help: The compound that was measured.
    %%field AssayResult.value help: The measured number, in the unit beside it.
    %%field AssayResult.unit help: The unit of the measurement. Kept on the result as well as the assay because units are occasionally reported differently for the same assay.
    %%field AssayResult.qualifier help: Whether the value is exact or a bound. A compound too weak to measure gives a greater-than, and treating that as an exact number is a classic way to mislead a project.
    %%field AssayResult.replicate_count help: How many replicates the value is averaged over.
    %%field AssayResult.standard_deviation help: The spread across replicates, which is how much of the value to trust.
    %%field AssayResult.is_validated help: Whether a scientist has reviewed and accepted this result.

    %%entity AdmetProfile help: What is known about whether a compound could behave like a drug — absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion and toxicity. Kept apart from Compound because each endpoint is measured separately, at different times, by different people, and a potent compound can be killed by any one of them.
    %%entity AdmetProfile parent: Compound
    %%field AdmetProfile.compound_id help: The compound this profile describes.
    %%field AdmetProfile.kinetic_solubility_um help: How much of the compound will dissolve, in micromolar. A compound that will not dissolve cannot be dosed and often cannot even be assayed honestly.
    %%field AdmetProfile.caco2_permeability help: Apparent permeability through a Caco-2 cell monolayer, the standard laboratory stand-in for absorption across the gut wall.
    %%field AdmetProfile.microsomal_half_life_min help: How long the compound survives in liver microsomes, in minutes. Short half-lives mean the liver clears the drug before it can work.
    %%field AdmetProfile.herg_ic50_um help: Potency against the hERG cardiac ion channel. Here a high number is the good outcome: hERG is an antitarget, and blocking it causes arrhythmia.
    %%field AdmetProfile.cyp3a4_inhibition_percent help: How much the compound inhibits CYP3A4, the enzyme that metabolises much of the pharmacopoeia. Strong inhibition means drug-drug interactions.
    %%field AdmetProfile.plasma_protein_bound_percent help: The percentage bound to plasma proteins. Only the unbound fraction is free to act, so a highly bound compound needs a much higher dose.
    %%field AdmetProfile.is_bbb_penetrant help: Whether the compound crosses the blood-brain barrier. Required for a neuroscience programme and usually unwanted everywhere else.
    %%field AdmetProfile.ames_result help: The outcome of the Ames mutagenicity test. A positive is normally the end of the compound.
    %%field AdmetProfile.assessed_on help: The date this profile was compiled.
    %%field AdmetProfile.assessed_by_id help: The DMPK scientist who compiled it.

    %% ---- Laboratory ---------------------------------------------------------
    %%entity Experiment help: A planned piece of laboratory work, reviewed and approved before it runs. The experiment is what instrument bookings, outsourced requests, samples and deviations all hang from, so it is the record an auditor follows to reconstruct what was done.
    %%field Experiment.title help: What the experiment is called.
    %%field Experiment.status help: Where the experiment has reached in its lifecycle, from draft to completed.
    %%field Experiment.program_id help: The programme the work is being done for.
    %%field Experiment.pi_id help: The principal investigator accountable for the experiment.
    %%field Experiment.project_code help: The code the work is charged to.
    %%field Experiment.estimated_duration_days help: How many days the work is expected to take. Required before the experiment may be submitted.
    %%field Experiment.is_gmp_critical help: Whether the work falls under good manufacturing practice. It raises the bar on every deviation raised against it.
    %%field Experiment.submitted_at help: When the experiment was submitted for review.
    %%field Experiment.completed_at help: When the work finished.
    %%field Experiment.objective help: What the experiment is meant to establish.

    %%entity Sample help: A physical quantity of material in a tube, plate or vial, in a known place. A sample is the thing a scientist can actually pick up, and the difference between the compound and the sample is the difference between a structure and eight microlitres of it in freezer A.
    %%field Sample.sample_barcode help: The barcode on the physical container. Scanning it is how the material is identified at the bench.
    %%field Sample.compound_id help: The compound this material is, where it is a registered compound.
    %%field Sample.experiment_id help: The experiment the sample was prepared for.
    %%field Sample.volume_ul help: Volume on hand, in microlitres.
    %%field Sample.mass_mg help: Mass on hand, in milligrams.
    %%field Sample.concentration_mm help: Concentration of the solution, in millimolar.
    %%field Sample.physical_state help: What form the material is in, which decides how it can be handled.
    %%field Sample.storage_location help: Where the sample physically is — the freezer, shelf and rack.
    %%field Sample.storage_temperature help: The temperature the sample must be kept at.
    %%field Sample.expiration_date help: The date after which the material should not be trusted.
    %%field Sample.status help: Whether the sample is available, spoken for, used up or quarantined.

    %%entity InventoryTransaction help: One movement of material — received, split into aliquots, consumed, moved, discarded or corrected. Samples are edited constantly, and a stock figure nobody can explain is worse than none, so every change to a sample leaves one of these behind.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.sample_id help: The sample that moved.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.performed_by_id help: Who moved it. Empty when the movement was recorded automatically by the disposition process rather than by a person at the bench.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.transaction_type help: What kind of movement this was.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.quantity_change help: How much material was added or removed. Negative for a consumption.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.from_location help: Where the material was before.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.to_location help: Where it went.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.occurred_at help: When the movement happened, where that is known separately from when the row was written.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.reason help: Why, where the movement needs explaining.

    %%entity Instrument help: A piece of laboratory equipment that produces data. Calibration is the field that matters: an overdue instrument invalidates everything measured on it, which is why a booking is refused rather than warned about.
    %%field Instrument.asset_tag help: The asset tag physically stuck to the instrument.
    %%field Instrument.name help: What people call it.
    %%field Instrument.instrument_type help: What kind of instrument it is.
    %%field Instrument.calibration_status help: Whether calibration is current, approaching, overdue or the instrument is out of service.
    %%field Instrument.last_calibration_date help: When it was last calibrated.
    %%field Instrument.next_calibration_date help: When calibration falls due.
    %%field Instrument.location help: Which laboratory it lives in.
    %%field Instrument.is_active help: Whether the instrument is in service. An inactive instrument cannot be booked.

    %%entity InstrumentBooking help: A reserved block of time on an instrument. Instruments are the scarce resource in most laboratories, so the booking is where contention is resolved and where an overdue calibration stops work before it starts.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.instrument_id help: The instrument being booked.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.experiment_id help: The experiment the time is for.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.booked_by_id help: Who made the booking.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.start_time help: When the block starts.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.end_time help: When it ends.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.status help: Whether the booking is pending, confirmed, running, finished or cancelled.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.cancellation_reason help: Why the booking was cancelled, where it was.

    %% ---- Quality and suppliers ----------------------------------------------
    %%entity Vendor help: An external supplier or contract research organisation. Much of discovery chemistry and biology is outsourced, so a vendor's approval status and service tier decide what work may be sent where.
    %%field Vendor.name help: The organisation's name.
    %%field Vendor.vendor_type help: What the vendor does for you.
    %%field Vendor.sla_tier help: The service level agreed with them, which sets the turnaround you can rely on.
    %%field Vendor.contact_email help: The address work is arranged through.
    %%field Vendor.contact_phone help: A number for when email is too slow.
    %%field Vendor.country help: Where the vendor operates, which affects shipping and what may be sent.
    %%field Vendor.is_approved help: Whether the vendor has passed qualification. Unapproved vendors may not be sent work.

    %%entity VendorRequest help: A piece of work sent outside — a synthesis, a panel, an analysis. It is raised against an experiment so that outsourced results can be traced back to the work that needed them.
    %%field VendorRequest.request_number help: The reference the request is tracked by with the vendor.
    %%field VendorRequest.vendor_id help: Who the work was sent to.
    %%field VendorRequest.experiment_id help: The experiment the work belongs to.
    %%field VendorRequest.raised_by_id help: Who raised the request.
    %%field VendorRequest.description help: What was asked for.
    %%field VendorRequest.quoted_cost help: The price quoted for the work.
    %%field VendorRequest.required_date help: The date the results are needed.
    %%field VendorRequest.status help: Where the request has got to.

    %%entity DeviationReport help: A record that something did not go to plan. Deviations are the raw material of laboratory quality: severity is assigned by rule rather than by opinion, and a critical one opens a corrective action automatically.
    %%field DeviationReport.experiment_id help: The experiment the deviation happened in.
    %%field DeviationReport.reported_by_id help: Who reported it.
    %%field DeviationReport.severity help: How serious it is. Assigned by the routing rule rather than chosen by the reporter.
    %%field DeviationReport.status help: Where the investigation has reached.
    %%field DeviationReport.anomaly_description help: What happened, in enough detail for somebody who was not there.
    %%field DeviationReport.anomaly_timestamp help: When it happened, as distinct from when it was reported.
    %%field DeviationReport.instrument_failure_involved help: Whether equipment was implicated, which raises the severity and usually the scope.

    %%entity CAPA help: A corrective and preventive action — what was done about a deviation, and what stops it happening again. A CAPA is closed only when its effectiveness has been verified, which is the whole difference between fixing an incident and fixing a process.
    %%field CAPA.deviation_report_id help: The deviation this action answers.
    %%field CAPA.remediation_owner_id help: Who owns getting it done.
    %%field CAPA.title help: A short description of the action.
    %%field CAPA.root_cause_category help: What the investigation concluded was the underlying cause.
    %%field CAPA.status help: Where the action has reached. Closing requires verification.
    %%field CAPA.required_resolution_date help: The date the action must be complete by, set from the severity.
    %%field CAPA.effectiveness_metric help: The measure used to judge whether the fix worked.
    %%field CAPA.remediation_plan help: What is actually going to be done.

    %% ---- People -------------------------------------------------------------
    %%entity User help: Somebody who works in the laboratory or on the portfolio. The role is what decides which of the entities above they can see at all.
    %%field User.email help: Their work address, which is also how they sign in.
    %%field User.first_name help: Given name.
    %%field User.last_name help: Family name.
    %%field User.role help: Their function, which determines what this application shows them.
    %%field User.team_id help: The team they belong to.
    %%field User.department help: The department they sit in.
    %%field User.is_active help: Whether they still work here.
    %%field User.last_login help: When they last signed in.

    %%entity Team help: A group of people who work together, usually around a technique or a programme.
    %%field Team.name help: What the team is called.
    %%field Team.manager_id help: Who runs it.
    %%field Team.department help: The department the team belongs to.

%% ---- Section 2: Business rule — experiment submission gate -----------------
%% What an experiment has to have before anybody's time is booked against it.
%% Every branch here is a refusal a reviewer would otherwise have to make by
%% hand, and each one names the field that is missing rather than saying no.
%%meta name: Experiment Submission Gate
%%meta kind: rules
%%rule experimentSubmitGate on Experiment event: beforeUpdate priority: 10
flowchart TD
    A([Start: Submit Requested]) --> B{Status == draft?}
    B -->|No| X1[Reject: Not in Draft]
    B -->|Yes| C{Title provided?}
    C -->|No| X2[Reject: Title Required]
    C -->|Yes| D{PI assigned?}
    D -->|No| X3[Reject: PI Required]
    D -->|Yes| E{Estimated duration >= 1?}
    E -->|No| X4[Reject: Duration Required]
    E -->|Yes| F{Programme assigned?}
    F -->|No| X5[Reject: Programme Required]
    F -->|Yes| P[Set status: submitted]
    X1 --> Z([End])
    X2 --> Z
    X3 --> Z
    X4 --> Z
    X5 --> Z
    P --> Z

%% ---- Section 3: Business rule — deviation severity routing -----------------
%% Severity is decided, not chosen. A reporter under pressure grades their own
%% mistake gently, so the grade is computed from the context — whether the work
%% was GMP-critical, whether equipment failed, how much was written down.
%%meta name: Deviation Severity Routing
%%meta kind: rules
%%rule deviationSeverity on DeviationReport event: beforeCreate priority: 10
flowchart TD
    A([Start: Deviation Reported]) --> B{GMP-critical experiment?}
    B -->|Yes| C{anomaly_description length >= 100?}
    C -->|Yes| H[Set severity: critical]
    C -->|No| M[Set severity: major]
    B -->|No| D{Instrument failure involved?}
    D -->|Yes| M2[Set severity: major]
    D -->|No| L[Set severity: minor]
    H --> N((Notify QA))
    M --> N
    M2 --> N
    L --> K((Notify Supervisor))
    N --> E([End: Routed])
    K --> E

    %% A critical deviation escalates: the action below names the multi-step
    %% workflow in Section 11, and its `when` is what keeps that workflow from
    %% running on every deviation.
    %%action escalateCritical trigger-workflow when: severity == "critical" workflow: CriticalDeviationEscalation message: Critical deviation — escalating and opening a CAPA

%% ---- Section 4: Business rule — instrument booking conflict check ----------
%% An overdue calibration is a refusal rather than a warning: data taken on an
%% uncalibrated instrument cannot be used, so letting the booking through only
%% wastes the slot and the sample.
%%meta name: Instrument Booking Conflict
%%meta kind: rules
%%rule bookingConflict on InstrumentBooking event: beforeCreate priority: 5
flowchart TD
    A([Start: Booking Requested]) --> B{Instrument is_active?}
    B -->|No| X1[Reject: Instrument Inactive]
    B -->|Yes| C{calibration_status == overdue?}
    C -->|Yes| X2[Reject: Calibration Overdue]
    C -->|No| D{Timeslot available?}
    D -->|No| X3[Reject: Booking Conflict]
    D -->|Yes| P[Confirm Booking]
    X1 --> Z([End])
    X2 --> Z
    X3 --> Z
    P --> Z

%% ---- Section 5: Workflow — programme lifecycle (state machine) --------------
%% A programme advances one stage at a time and can stop at any of them. The
%% edges that are missing are the point: there is no route from hit
%% identification straight to candidate selection, because skipping lead
%% optimization is not a decision anyone is allowed to take by editing a field.
%%meta name: Programme Lifecycle
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow ProgramLifecycle entity: Program kind: state
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> proposed
    proposed     --> active       : fund
    active       --> on_hold      : pause
    on_hold      --> active       : resume
    active       --> completed    : nominate_candidate
    active       --> discontinued : discontinue
    on_hold      --> discontinued : discontinue
    completed    --> [*]
    discontinued --> [*]

    %%rbac role:project_manager|lab_manager on Program.fund
    %%rbac role:project_manager on Program.nominate_candidate
    %%rbac role:project_manager|lab_manager on Program.discontinue
    %%trigger webhook:portfolio -> syncProgramStage on Program

%% ---- Section 6: Workflow — experiment lifecycle (state machine) -------------
%%meta name: Experiment Lifecycle
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow ExperimentLifecycle entity: Experiment kind: state
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> draft
    draft        --> submitted   : submit
    submitted    --> in_review   : assign_reviewer
    submitted    --> draft       : return_to_draft
    in_review    --> approved    : approve
    in_review    --> draft       : reject
    approved     --> in_progress : start
    in_progress  --> completed   : complete
    in_progress  --> on_hold     : pause
    on_hold      --> in_progress : resume
    in_progress  --> cancelled   : cancel
    approved     --> cancelled   : cancel
    completed    --> [*]
    cancelled    --> [*]

    %%rbac role:biologist|medicinal_chemist|lab_manager on Experiment.submit
    %%rbac role:quality_assurance|lab_manager on Experiment.approve
    %%rbac role:lab_manager on Experiment.cancel
    %%trigger webhook:lims -> syncExperimentStatus on Experiment

%% ---- Section 7: Workflow — CAPA lifecycle (state machine) ------------------
%%meta name: CAPA Lifecycle
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow CapaLifecycle entity: CAPA kind: state
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> open
    open                 --> in_progress          : assign
    in_progress          --> pending_verification : submit_for_review
    pending_verification --> closed               : verify_effective
    pending_verification --> in_progress          : reopen
    in_progress          --> cancelled            : cancel
    closed               --> [*]
    cancelled            --> [*]

    %%rbac role:quality_assurance on CAPA.verify_effective
    %%rbac role:quality_assurance|lab_manager on CAPA.cancel
    %%trigger cron:0 8 * * * -> escalateOverdueCapa on CAPA

%% ---- Section 8: Workflow — compound registration hooks ---------------------
%% Registration is where a structure becomes a record, and it is the only place
%% the calculated properties are computed. Doing it here rather than on read
%% means every consumer of the row sees the same numbers.
%%meta name: Compound Registration
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow CompoundRegistration entity: Compound kind: hook
flowchart TD
    A[Client Request] --> B[Validate SMILES]
    B --> C[customValidate: validateSmilesFormat]
    C --> D[beforeCreate: generateInchiKey]
    D --> E[beforeCreate: computeMolecularProperties]
    E --> F[beforeCreate: countLipinskiViolations]
    F --> G[Process Compound]
    G --> H[afterCreate: indexForStructureSearch]
    H --> I[afterCreate: notifyRegistrationTeam]
    I --> J[Response]

    %%hook customValidate validateSmilesFormat on Compound[field: smiles]
    %%hook beforeCreate generateInchiKey on Compound[field: inchi_key]
    %%hook beforeCreate computeMolecularProperties on Compound[field: molecular_weight]
    %%hook beforeCreate countLipinskiViolations on Compound[field: lipinski_violations]
    %%hook afterCreate indexForStructureSearch on Compound
    %%hook afterCreate notifyRegistrationTeam on Compound

%% ---- Section 9: Workflow — assay result validation hooks -------------------
%% The quality gate on a number. A result is checked against the run it came
%% from before it is stored, because a result from a plate that failed QC is
%% worse than no result at all — it looks like data.
%%meta name: Assay Result Validation
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow AssayResultValidation entity: AssayResult kind: hook
flowchart TD
    A[Client Request] --> B[Validate Request]
    B --> C[customValidate: rejectResultsFromFailedRun]
    C --> D[beforeCreate: normaliseUnit]
    D --> E[Process Result]
    E --> F[afterCreate: recomputeSeriesPotency]
    F --> G[afterCreate: flagPotencyOutliers]
    G --> H[Response]

    %%hook customValidate rejectResultsFromFailedRun on AssayResult[field: assay_run_id]
    %%hook beforeCreate normaliseUnit on AssayResult[field: unit]
    %%hook afterCreate recomputeSeriesPotency on AssayResult
    %%hook afterCreate flagPotencyOutliers on AssayResult

%% ---- Section 10: Workflow — deviation report hooks -------------------------
%%meta name: Deviation Report Hooks
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow DeviationReportHooks entity: DeviationReport kind: hook
flowchart TD
    A[Client Request] --> B[Validate]
    B --> C[beforeCreate: timestampAnomaly]
    C --> D[customValidate: requireDescription]
    D --> E[Process Deviation]
    E --> F[afterCreate: triggerCapaIfCritical]
    F --> G[afterCreate: notifyQualityTeam]
    G --> H[Response]

    %%hook beforeCreate timestampAnomaly on DeviationReport[field: anomaly_timestamp]
    %%hook customValidate requireDescription on DeviationReport[field: anomaly_description]
    %%hook afterCreate triggerCapaIfCritical on DeviationReport
    %%hook afterCreate notifyQualityTeam on DeviationReport

%% ---- Section 11: Workflow — critical deviation escalation ------------------
%% A multi-step process: it decides an SLA from a table, computes from what the
%% table published, creates a CAPA in another entity, updates the record that
%% triggered it, and then reaches back into the row it created using the id that
%% step bound. `trigger: rule` is what makes the severity condition in Section 3
%% load-bearing — without it the workflow would run on every deviation, critical
%% or not.
%%
%% Step B is a decision table living inside the process. The base SLA used to be
%% a literal 3, which meant the number was in the diagram but the reasoning
%% behind it was nowhere: changing the window for major deviations meant editing
%% a Formula and hoping the reader inferred why. As a table, the policy is the
%% artifact, and the two variables it publishes are read by the steps after it.
%%meta name: Critical Deviation Escalation
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow CriticalDeviationEscalation entity: DeviationReport kind: saga trigger: rule
flowchart TD
    A([Critical deviation reported]) --> B{Classify the severity}
    B --> C[Compute the resolution window]
    C --> D[Open a CAPA]
    D --> E[Escalate the deviation]
    E --> F[Record the window on the CAPA]
    F --> G([Escalated])

    %%step B Decision decisionTable: {"hitPolicy":"first","inputs":[{"id":"i1","name":"Severity","field":"severity"}],"outputs":[{"id":"o1","name":"BaseDays","field":"baseDays"},{"id":"o2","name":"Category","field":"capaCategory"}],"rules":[{"_id":"critical","i1":"\"critical\"","o1":"1","o2":"'immediate_containment'"},{"_id":"major","i1":"\"major\"","o1":"3","o2":"'under_investigation'"},{"_id":"rest","i1":"","o1":"7","o2":"'under_investigation'"}]}
    %%step C Formula target: resolutionDays source: baseDays operation: multiply operand: 7
    %%step D CreateEntity entity: CAPA as: newCapaId fields: {"deviation_report_id":"id","title":"CAPA for a critical deviation","root_cause_category":"capaCategory","remediation_owner_id":"reported_by_id","required_resolution_date":"anomaly_timestamp","status":"open"}
    %%step E UpdateEntity field: status value: escalated
    %%step F UpdateEntity entity: CAPA targetSource: newCapaId field: effectiveness_metric source: resolutionDays

%% ---- Section 12: Workflow — sample disposition on update -------------------
%% The escalation in Section 11 is rule-triggered: a rule decides whether it
%% runs at all. This one is the other half of the pairing — it runs on the
%% lifecycle itself, every time a sample is updated, and carries its own
%% decision instead of being gated by one.
%%
%% What it writes is an InventoryTransaction, which is the honest destination: a
%% sample whose status changes has moved, been used up or been quarantined, and
%% a stock figure nobody can explain is worse than no stock figure. The ledger
%% row is created first, then the sample is moved, then the row is completed
%% with where it went — create, update and update-across-entities in one process.
%%meta name: Sample Disposition
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow SampleDisposition entity: Sample kind: saga trigger: automatic operation: UPDATE
flowchart TD
    A([Sample updated]) --> B{Decide the disposition}
    B --> C[Open a ledger entry]
    C --> D[Move the sample]
    D --> E[Complete the ledger entry]
    E --> F([Disposed])

    %%step B Decision decisionTable: {"hitPolicy":"first","inputs":[{"id":"i1","name":"Status","field":"status"}],"outputs":[{"id":"o1","name":"MovementType","field":"dispositionType"},{"id":"o2","name":"Location","field":"dispositionLocation"}],"rules":[{"_id":"consumed","i1":"\"consumed\"","o1":"'consumption'","o2":"'ARCHIVE-A'"},{"_id":"quarantined","i1":"\"quarantined\"","o1":"'transfer'","o2":"'QUARANTINE-1'"},{"_id":"disposed","i1":"\"disposed\"","o1":"'disposal'","o2":"'WASTE'"},{"_id":"rest","i1":"","o1":"'adjustment'","o2":"'FREEZER-A'"}]}
    %%step C CreateEntity entity: InventoryTransaction as: ledgerEntryId fields: {"sample_id":"id","transaction_type":"dispositionType","from_location":"storage_location"}
    %%step D UpdateEntity field: storage_location source: dispositionLocation
    %%step E UpdateEntity entity: InventoryTransaction targetSource: ledgerEntryId field: to_location source: dispositionLocation

%% ---- Section 13: Workflow — assay retirement on delete ---------------------
%% The third trigger. Section 11 runs when a rule names it, Section 12 runs on
%% every update, and this one runs when a record is deleted.
%%
%% A delete is a hard delete, and the row goes last: this workflow runs inside
%% the same transaction, before the removal, which is the only order that works.
%% An Assay owns its runs, so the runs have to be cleared while the assay still
%% exists — a foreign key does not care that we intended to delete the parent.
%% If the cascade throws, nothing is deleted and the assay is still there to try
%% again.
%%
%%meta name: Assay Retirement
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow AssayRetirement entity: Assay kind: saga trigger: automatic operation: DELETE
flowchart TD
    A([Assay deleted]) --> B{Classify the retirement}
    B --> C[Record the retirement]
    C --> D[Clear the runs]
    D --> E([Retired])

    %%step B Decision decisionTable: {"hitPolicy":"first","inputs":[{"id":"i1","name":"Active","field":"is_active"}],"outputs":[{"id":"o1","name":"Reason","field":"retirementReason"}],"rules":[{"_id":"inactive","i1":"false","o1":"'retired_after_decommission'"},{"_id":"rest","i1":"","o1":"'retired_early'"}]}
    %%step C UpdateEntity field: protocol_reference source: retirementReason
    %%step D DeleteEntity entity: AssayRun targetField: assay_id

